# 
# ==============================================================================
# STEP 11 -- ANALYSIS 11: STRUCTURAL BREAK -- UNKNOWN BREAKPOINT SEARCH
# Annual multi-crop panel: TMX_ann, PRE_ann, no phenology, no VPD
#
# BUG FIX (this version): the sample filter was missing `area_harvested_ha
# > 0`, the same class of error found and fixed in Steps 04-09. Filter now
# matches Step03's GDP-sample construction exactly. An N cross-check
# against Step03's saved N_M4 has been added at the start of each crop's
# loop iteration.
#
# Extends Step06's single-year (1990) Chow-type test to a Quandt-Andrews
# style SEARCH over every candidate break year, following the standard
# 15% trimming rule (Andrews 1993): the first and last 15% of each crop's
# observed year range are excluded as candidates, since a break estimated
# too close to either end of the sample is unreliable (too few
# observations on one side).
#
# For each candidate year tau, the same interaction specification as
# Step06 is estimated:
#   ln_yield ~ TMX_c + PRE_c + IRR + ln_gdp + TMX_c:post + PRE_c:post
#              | Country_ISO + Yil
#   where post = 1 if Yil >= tau
# and the joint Wald statistic on (TMX_c:post, PRE_c:post) is recorded.
# tau* = argmax(Wald statistic) is the data-driven "most likely" break year.
#
# IMPORTANT STATISTICAL CAVEAT (explicitly reported, not hidden):
# because ~40+ candidate years are tested and the single strongest one is
# selected, the RAW p-value at tau* is NOT a valid significance test for
# that specific year (classic multiple-testing/pretesting problem). This
# step reports the search process transparently (Wald statistic across
# all candidate years, plus tau*) as an EXPLORATORY result. A corrected
# p-value (Andrews 1993 critical values or bootstrap) is a follow-up step,
# only worth doing if this exploratory search shows a genuinely promising,
# cross-crop-consistent pattern.
#
# Plots: G_BK1 (Wald statistic by candidate year, 8-crop facet, tau*
#        marked), G_BK2 (tau* summary across crops)
#
# References:
#   Andrews (1993, Econometrica) -- unknown breakpoint test, 15% trimming
#   Quandt (1960)                -- sup-Wald breakpoint search logic
# ==============================================================================

